g with  ukraine now  trying to take back a former state that  largely wants to be independent of  russian control  2014 is a good year to look at the  relationship between ukraine and russia  the then president of ukraine with a  close relationship to russia faced huge  protests demanding he quit and when he  did russia's vladimir putin thought that  ukraine was about to become more  friendly with western countries like the  uk and the usa  so he sent in russian soldiers to take  control of crimea which is an area in  the south of ukraine  let's travel in time right up to the  present day the ukraine invasion is now  a very complicated situation  rebels are people who go against the  government in power  in february russia sent in its troops to  support a rebel army from two other  regions who also wanted to be  independent from ukraine lehansk and  danesque  so rebels with russians began fighting  against the ukrainian army  but the most important thing to know is  that this was just the start of a  full-scale invasion of ukraine  president putin appeared on russian tv  to want other countries not to try and  stop them  there is huge support for ukraine here  in the west for all his bombs and tanks  and missiles  i don't believe that the russian  dictator will ever subdue the national  feeling of the ukrainians  and their passionate belief that their  country should be free just like other  former soviet union states the ukraine  is an independent country wants to join  the military alliance nato  and the european union  but the russian president is demanding  ukraine doesn't join them  there are still large russian  communities in many former soviet states  including ukraine  while some would like to be part of  russia world leaders say president putin  has no right to weighed in  and many say this invasion is illegal  and russia will be punished  one thing you can be sure about is that  the world has been watching events in  ukraine very closely and those in power  are doing all they can to bring about a  peaceful solution and i've been looking  into how the world is reacting  [Music]  for weeks many world leaders and senior  politicians tried to convince russia's  president putin that an invasion of  ukraine was not what ukrainians wanted  or even his own people and that it would  be viewed as illegal and russia would be  punished  but he sent his forces across the  ukrainian border and began an unprovoked  attack on the country bombing military  targets in key cities including the  capital kiev  so what happens  next the many families taking shelter in  underground stations and bunkers from  the gunfire and explosions know the  world is watching and reacting  [Music]  military action by other countries has  been ruled out that could lead to a much  bigger war  instead european countries including the  uk are providing the ukrainian military  with supplies and weapons  no one really knows why but the russian  advance in the early days of the  invasion has been slow the ukrainian  army is fighting back hard led by their  president vladimir zelensky  he urged russians not to listen to their  president  we need to stop this war  we can live in peace together globally  as humans  world leaders including the uk prime  minister have described the invasion as  a pointless war and they're punishing  the russian president with financial  penalties called sanctions which means  they can't do business outside russia  and won't be able to access money or  property abroad  russian airlines are banned from flying  across or landing in europe  this is all to try and cut russia off  from the outside world to stop its  actions  the world of sport has also stepped in  the football governing bodies uefa and  fifa have suspended russia from  competitions and many other sporting  events due to be held in russia have  also been cancelled russia has also been  banned from this year's eurovision song  contest  many ukrainians are staying put in the  country instead of fleeing the invasion  we've got a video diary from miroslava  who lives in kelmanitsky she is among  loads of teenagers who have stayed  behind to provide food and aid to  refugees as they make their way through  the country to the border  right here people are making nuts which  serve as  a masking equipment for our military  so right here the food has been  collected  and  over there our volunteers are working  and collecting  some other stuff  and medicament  for our soldiers i've never seen so many  volunteers in one place  miroslava joins us now  hi maurice lava thank you for joining us  hi guys thank you for making this  program and for speaking to me  why have you not fled ukraine why are  you staying behind to help those who are  where i am now we have several uh air  raid sirens per day and people have to  spend  some part of the day hiding in the  underground shelters the situation is  relatively safer than in kim or  in hakeev but still we are helping  people who are coming to khmer natsuki  as  refugees we are also sending the  humanitarian aid to the front line and  it's it's truly horrible to see our  mothers with their toddlers coming to  manlinski only  with one back  with their documents  with no extra worm clothes and we are  trying to provide them on the food  and  we are hosting them in our own homes in  schools  sports centers cinemas and even in  churches but it's scary to  to think that uh tomorrow you may become  one of those refugees too  well thank you so much for speaking to  us and i really hope you stay safe i  want to thank you and thank all of our  foreign friends for supporting us and i  want to ask you to continue supporting  us  we've been told by refugee charities  that an estimated 4 to 7 million people  are expected to flee the fighting  already hundreds of thousands of  refugees are crossing the border into  neighbouring countries that's moldova  romania hungary slovakia and poland  refugee charities are warning that there  could be a humanitarian crisis if those  countries don't get the support they  need to take care of ukraine's refugees  the good news is that the european union  has announced that all ukrainian  refugees will be allowed to stay in safe  countries like poland for up to three  years  the first thing you notice is that it is  mainly women and children crossing over  the border into neighboring countries  that is because the ukrainian government  has asked men to stay behind and fight  katarina who is 18 had her brother 12  year old vadim have just arrived in  poland  they told fyi their parents like many  others have stayed behind to help fight  the invasion they stay  there to defend our country they want it  for us to come there and  now we are here it's really bad  so i must say that  [Music]  we have really warm in our country  children  can't do nothing so  i um  really beg uh adults to protest in such  way in  anything  uh to stop this world to stop this  really  bad thing in our country  we were so worried about katarina and  vadham that we've kept in touch with  them and they told fyi that they've now  reached friends of the family in  slovakia  all the refugees are being offered  shelter by local families in their homes  and temporary accommodation in halls and  schools  they get there by car train or on fort  there are so many refugees some are  waiting 30 hours to cross  anna alabed knows what it takes to  travel hundreds of miles to reach safety  she's a refugee from syria where a civil  war broke out between government and  anti-government troops forcing millions  of people to flee  she became well known for her tweets as  her city was bombed by her own  government and its supporter russia  banner's family escaped to neighboring  turkey banner has been posted again  about her concern for ukrainian refugees  and she's joining us now hi banner  hi so it must be so tough for you to see  yet another refugee crisis do you think  that they will get the help they need i  i think they will get the help they need  if the world all focuses on one goal and  that is to bring peace and to stop these  words because every child deserves to  live in peace and  leaving your whole country because of  war is really tough because you need to  find a new home you need to find a new  school you need to find a new friend and  that is really tough and i really hope  that  everybody will fix this thing and the  war will stop and  the people will go back to their  homelands and live in peace thank you so  much banner  countries around the world are sending  money food and water to help refugees  including the uk  many of us are wanting to do what we can  to help donating whatever we can to  collection centres across the country  thomas has more on this from london i'm  here at the pogus centre in lewisham in  london this is one of the drop-off  points that has been set up across the  country to help ukrainian refugees as  you can see it is extremely busy with  boxes being put into trucks  so why did you volunteer today because  ukraine  needs help people may be starving  they've been walking  country to country every day  it's really hectic here there are so  many people and so many donations some  of this being kids cloves food nappies  sanitary products and that's just a few  of them we have been packing all the  stuff that people have been donating and  putting it in trucks by going to poland  have you got the truck coming keep  packing keep packing  we've been here for a few hours  everyone's been working flat out and  there's already another truck here for  loading  while more trucks like these packed full  of aid we'll keep heading to border  countries of ukraine as long as we keep  donating whatever we can back to you  guys  thanks thomas now i suppose it's the  least we can do as we watch these events  unfold in ukraine and watching the news  can be scary sometimes can't it so we  invited members of fyi school news clubs  to send their questions and worries into  fyi's counsellor anna  is it okay to feel scared about what's  happening in ukraine even though i'm not  there  so if you are feeling any worrying  feelings or feeling scared you really  aren't on your own okay it's because we  feel empathy and sympathy for those  people that are caught up in all of this  conflict so yeah if you are feeling sad  or upset it is very very normal but  don't sit with those feelings okay make  sure you talk to someone particularly an  adult that you trust share that emotion  don't just be sat with it  i feel really anxious i'm worried about  the war and what it might mean for us  could this lead to another world war  it's natural to be worried about what  will happen next and be careful as well  on what new sites you're accessing don't  keep scrolling through unreliable  sources that are perhaps scam mongering  or dishing out incorrect information  only go to reputable news sites in order  to get the correct news and what is  actually happening when you do you'll  realize that world leaders are doing  everything they need to do to avoid this  war getting worse and affecting more  countries you can turn that concern as  well into doing something for charity do  something proactive like sending  donations to a collection center for  refugees just like thomas has just seen  people from all countries all around the  world have come together to protest  against russia's invasion of ukraine  including russia itself which is brave  because protests aren't allowed there we  leave you with pictures of a world  united in shock but also in hope for  peace  [Music]  [Music]  you

